<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Girder.Http</PackageId>
    <Description>The request-pipeline pieces that need no provider: correlation ids, rate limiting, and the caller's address. Depends on ASP.NET and Girder.Abstractions, and on nothing else.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Why this is its own package.

    Girder.Infrastructure carries twenty-one third-party dependencies —
    Swashbuckle, OpenTelemetry, nine Serilog packages, JWT bearer, TOTP,
    FluentValidation. That is right for a service that runs the whole default
    set. It is wrong for a gateway that only routes and wants two middlewares:
    it would inherit Swagger and a telemetry pipeline to get a correlation id.

    Measured on one such gateway, that cost was enough to make its author write
    both middlewares by hand instead — twenty lines of correlation and a rate
    limiter, maintained forever, because taking Girder's was the more expensive
    option. A library that makes its own use the expensive path has failed at
    the thing it exists for.

    So: everything here needs ASP.NET and Girder.Abstractions and nothing else.
    Anything that needs a provider, a serialiser or a telemetry stack belongs in
    Girder.Infrastructure, which references this and re-exposes it unchanged.
  -->

  <!--
    THE NAMESPACES DO NOT CHANGE. They stay `Girder.Infrastructure.*`, which
    reads oddly in a package called Girder.Http and is still right: moving types
    between assemblies keeps every `using` compiling, while renaming the
    namespace would break the source of every caller. For a minor version that
    is the wrong price, and the tidier name is not worth it.
  -->

  <!--
    One framework reference and no package reference at all. The memory cache the
    in-process rate limit counter builds on is part of the shared framework, so
    naming it would only add a line the build rejects as redundant.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Girder.Abstractions/Girder.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
